  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "In a gas station, $55%$ of the customers buy regular gasoline while $45%$ buy premium gasoline. If $60%$ of the customers that buy regular gasoline usually pay with a credit card and the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $0.645,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected customer who buys premium gasoline usually pays with a credit card? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person pays with a credit card, and let $R$ be the event that the person buys regular gas. Then ${R}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the person buys premium gas.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person pays with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{\u2032})P({R}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $55%$ of the customers buy regular gasoline while $45%$ buy premium gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.55,\\,P({R}^{\u2032})=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $60%$ of the customers that buy regular gasoline pay with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.60.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected customer pays with a credit card is $0.645,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(C)=0.645.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(C|{R}^{\u2032}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{\u2032})P({R}^{\u2032}) \\\\ 0.645 & =(0.60)(0.55)+P(C|{R}^{\u2032})(0.45) \\\\ 0.645 & =0.33+0.45P(C|{R}^{\u2032}) \\\\ 0.45P(C|{R}^{\u2032}) & =0.315 \\\\ P(C|{R}^{\u2032}) & =0.70.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected customer who buys premium gasoline usually pays with a credit card is $0.70.$",
